# `i % n`: beside a number the remainder is made on numbers: every turn asks the divisor again for an int32 that is neither negative nor zero, and what comes out is a double whatever came in, so that the sum is made on doubles too.
# No turn changes the divisor. In front of the loop, which is built twice, it is asked once for an int32 of at least 1: under that type the remainder of an integer that is not negative is an integer by the rule of forms,
# and the sum follows it. Whatever else the divisor is (zero, negative, a fraction, NaN) takes the other copy, which is as it was.
# function sum(n: number) { let s = 0; for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) s = (s + i % n) | 0; return s; }
# (globalThis as any).kept = sum;
# console.log(sum(7));
